{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"tremendous-penniless-queensnake","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## The defect\n\n`REBAR_MCP_READONLY=` (set to the EMPTY STRING) silently overrides an explicit\n`[mcp] readonly = true` in `rebar.toml` and brings the MCP server up with its full write surface.\nNo error, no warning. Every OTHER malformed value is loud.\n\n## Reproduced by me, independently, against current `origin/main`\n\nWith a `rebar.toml` containing `[mcp]\\nreadonly = true`:\n\n```\nREBAR_MCP_READONLY unset      -> mcp_readonly() = True    (file honoured)\nREBAR_MCP_READONLY=''         -> mcp_readonly() = False   <-- file DEFEATED, silently\nREBAR_MCP_READONLY='  '       -> mcp_readonly() = False\nREBAR_MCP_READONLY='garbage'  -> ConfigError: cannot resolve the MCP read-only gate ... (loud)\nREBAR_MCP_READONLY='false'    -> mcp_readonly() = False   (correct, explicit)\n```\n\n## Mechanism, verified in source\n\n- `src/rebar/_config_coercion.py` — `_FALSE = {\"false\", \"0\", \"no\", \"off\", \"\"}`. The empty string is\n  an accepted spelling of False.\n- `src/rebar/_config_sources.py:398` — the env override gates on key PRESENCE (`if name in\n  os.environ`), not on the value being non-empty. So `REBAR_MCP_READONLY=` enters the layer stack\n  as `\"\"`, and env outranks the file.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py:158` — `if ctx.readonly():` returns early and registers NO write tools.\n  So the gate genuinely controls the write surface; this is not a cosmetic flag.\n\nThis directly defeats the stated contract of `config.mcp_readonly()`, which documents itself as\nfail-loud per operator ruling `39f8-ae7c`: *\"a fault must error, never silently resolve to a\ndefault, even a safe one\"*. The empty string is the one input that resolves silently — and it\nresolves in the UNSAFE direction.\n\n## The asymmetry is what makes it a trap\n\nBoth gates coerce `\"\"` to `False`. But `False` means opposite things:\n\n```\nconfig: [mcp] readonly = true, allow_llm = true\nREBAR_MCP_READONLY=''   -> readonly=False  allow_llm=True    <-- protection OFF\nREBAR_MCP_ALLOW_LLM=''  -> readonly=True   allow_llm=False   <-- capability OFF (safe)\n```\n\nSo the identical operator mistake is harmless on one gate and disables a security control on the\nother. Anyone reasoning \"an empty env var is the same as unset\" is right for `allow_llm` and wrong\nfor `readonly`.\n\n## EXPOSURE: latent, NOT live — stated plainly because it changes the priority\n\nI checked the deployment before filing. **`REBAR_MCP_READONLY` is not set at all in\n`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml`**, and the only two places that set it —\n`.github/workflows/jira-dc-richtext-probe.yml:105` and `jira-dc-capability-map.yml:109` — pass a\nliteral `\"1\"`. There is no `${VAR}` indirection on it anywhere in `infra/` or `.github/`.\n\nSo no live instance exists today. The reported trigger (`REBAR_MCP_READONLY=${MCP_RO}` in a\ncompose/systemd/k8s env block with `MCP_RO` unset) is a plausible FUTURE shape, not the current\none. This is a latent footgun in a security control, filed at p2 on that basis rather than p1.\n\n## Compounding (verify before relying on it — I did not reproduce this half)\n\nThe sweep also reported `_config_sources.py` testing `REBAR_CONFIG_UNKNOWN_KEYS == \"error\"` only,\nso `strict`/`fail`/`1`/`true`/`yes` would all silently degrade to WARN — meaning an operator's\nattempt to make unknown keys loud is itself ineffective, and a typo'd `mcp.read_only` would be\ndropped with a log line while the server comes up writable. I have NOT verified that half; treat\nit as an unproven lead and confirm it before designing around it.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `REBAR_MCP_READONLY=` (empty) does NOT silently disable the read-only gate. Whether it\n      raises, or is treated as unset, is a design decision — make it deliberately and record the\n      reasoning; do not just add `\"\"` to `_TRUE`.\n- [ ] Whatever is chosen applies to the SECURITY-DIRECTION gates as a class, not just this one\n      key. Enumerate every boolean config gate whose `False` means \"protection off\" and state each\n      one's disposition — fixing only `readonly` leaves the next one to be found the same way.\n- [ ] `allow_llm` and other capability gates keep their current safe behaviour on `\"\"`. A fix that\n      makes an empty string raise everywhere may be right, but confirm it does not break the\n      deliberate \"unset means denied\" posture those gates rely on.\n- [ ] A regression test drives the real resolver (not a stub) with `\"\"`, `\"  \"`, unset, and a\n      malformed value against a file that sets the opposite, and pins each outcome. Seed the\n      defect by reverting the fix and confirm the test goes RED — an assertion that passes both\n      before and after would be this ticket's own bug class.\n- [ ] The empty-string coercion is examined once across ALL config keys, and any other key where\n      `\"\"` resolves in an unsafe direction is listed with its disposition.","id":"b2ff-2588-9bb7-4bd2","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"REBAR_MCP_READONLY= (empty string) silently defeats an explicit readonly=true and brings the MCP server up writable -- the one value that resolves silently, in the unsafe direction, on a gate documented as fail-loud"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788585139161546001,"uuid":"4c0b8ea0-130c-4741-a0b8-5db1d301bd67"}